Slither Game 

Slither is a two-player snake-style game developed for the Atmega32U2 microcontroller using the UCFK4 development board.
Players control their snakes on a 7×5 LED matrix, attempting to avoid collisions while trying to outlast their opponent. It has multiplayer gameplay using IR-UART for data transmission.

How to Play

Setup

Two UCFK4 boards are required.

Align the boards so their IR transmitters face each other.

Program both boards with the compiled game.out.

Controls

Use the navswitch to move your snake:

North: Up, South: Down, East: Right, West: Left

The snake’s position will update on the RED LED Screen, the snake is able to cross over between screens by simpling navigating over to it.

To grow the snake's length consuming an apple will increases the body length by 1 LED your length will differ on different screens and equal to how many apples you ate on that screen , to spawn in apples press down on the navswitch you can only spawn apples on your own screen.

you can hide in the LED strip for additional stragery.

How the snakes die

If the users snake's head hits the opponents opponets snake head the winner is given to whos screen the event occured on, If the user snake's head hits the opponent snake body this will be DEATH for the user and a LOSE this is vice versa for the opponent to win.
Users colliding with their own bodies will also result in death for the user and a win for the opponent. Additionally if user snake head collides with a wall user will lose and opponent will win.

Once a death scenario is detected, the board displays a “LOSE” or "WIN" message on the board where the death event happened.

Restarting

Press the reset button on both boards to restart the game through the terminal.

AI Statement of Use

